\f1\fs22 For a time, they went up to the mezzanine and stood at the railing and Elvi-watched with holding-hands eyes, which were also doctor eyes and investigator eyes.\
Hayley noticed the swell and the flow of the crowd, the stationary clumps and the two\'92s and three\'92s driving among them, the singles lifting around, not finding a group they felt welcome in, the watchers from the edges, trying to look casual leaning against a wall or sitting the arm of a couch. There but not there. Among all that, a force moved and disrupted all the stable patterns wherever it went.\
Chanta moved about with relative ease, people making way for him and his entourage of Elvisettes like water off the bow of a ship, excited in his wake, shoe following fro a while, others finding themselves in new clumps either toasting the High Elvis or mourning this they had lost.\
Kathy noticed a Clambake with grey coming through his sideburns hauling himself, with effort, off a Chesterfield, his hand lingering its arms while he got steady on his feet. An Elvine came off a bar stool and stood a moment with her palm pressed flat into the small of her back. A Rusty Martin stood up from a low stool in one go, still talking, and went to the bar.\
Behind Kathy and Hayley, a group of Elvines were catching up. The snippets Kathy caught earlier made it clear some hadn\'92t seen each other for years, or perhaps decades. Kathy tuned out until one of them squealed.\
\'93What happened to your scar?\'94 one blurted in disbelief.\
Kathy tuned while Hayley kept her security eye on the crowd below.\
\'93Look!\'94 a Maile Duval Elvine said and held out her arm. \'93Almost vanished.\'94\
The first Elvine examined her friend\'92s arm. \'93That\'92s amazing! You had that since we were fourteen.\'94\
Kathy stepped into the energetic bubble of the friends circle. \'93Do you mind if I take a look? I\'92m\'97\'94\
\'93Aunt Kathy! We know,\'94 Maile bubbled.\
\'93I mean, I\'92m doctor.\'94\
\'93Yes, we know that too,\'94 said Maile\'92s friend since they were fourteen as Maile put her arm into Kathy\'92s hands with the satisfaction of a woman who has finally found somebody qualified to agree with her.\
In the dim light of the room, Kathy pulled a medical flashlight from her lab coat\'97the good lab coat, the one she wore to any important event, such as nights at the opera or negations with \'91distributors\'92. She examined the arm, blinked, then crouched to inspect it closer.\
\'93Show me the scar,\'94 Kathy said, all doctor-professional.\
That made Hayley turn and pay attention.\
\'93I know, right?\'94 Maile pointed.\
Kathy only saw a little patchy redness, like a skin irritation. \'93How long ago was the accident?\'94\
\'93Twenty-five years I had it-Since I was fifteen\'97and now look!\'94\
Kathy was looking. The redness ran from Maile\'92s wrist to the inside of elbow and halfway to her armpit. Kathy doubted it was ever a serious thing, redder at the wrist and the high end, paler in the middle, like a word rubbed off a whiteboard by someone in a hurry.\
\'93How did it happen?\'94 asked Haley as she stood behind Kathy.\
\'93My boyfriend\'92s car caught fire,\'94 Maile said. \'93He\'92d done a show out of town and he was driving me home after. About halfway, it started smelling hot, and I said so.\'94\
\'93He said it was the road,\'94 Maile\'92s friend said, \'93but it was the brakes.\'94\
\'93Smoke got in and even then I had to beg him to stop. He pulled over and we stood in the grass and watched it go up.\'94 Maile turned her wrist over. \'93He sat down\'85 no shouting, no running about, no trying to save anything... he just gave up. I think he loved that car more than me.\'94 Then she added, \'93It was a pink and white Cadillac,\'94 as if that might explain how a boy could love a car more than a girlfriend. \'93He just sat down with his hands hanging between his knees and watched it, tears streaming down his face.\'94\
\'93Every single thing he owned was in it,\'94 Maile\'92s friend said. She could probably have told the whole tale herself.\
Hayley allowed a respectful pause for a loved car, then asked \'93How were you burned?\'94\
\'93She went in for the guitar,\'94 Maile\'92s friend said, shaking her head. She put her hand on Maile\'92s shoulder and rubbed.\
Maile was back at the car, inhabiting the memory. \'93I don\'92t even remember there being flames when I reached in to get the guitar. By the time I grabbed the case handle, I felt heat like my arm was in an oven. Next thing I\'92m standing six paces back with my blouse\'85 it was on fire.\'94\
\'93It was poly-cotton," her friend said. "That's what did it. The cotton takes hold and the poly won't let go of you.\'94\
\'93My blouse was pure cotton.\'94\
\'93Don\'92t give me that, girl. Our families couldn\'92t afford pure cotton.\'94\
\'93My momma saved for months for that blouse. I was so happy when she handed it down to me. It was cotton.\'94\
\'93You weren\'92t even there,\'94 Maile said.\
Even so faded, Kathy could see the colour was not even along it\'97paler in places, and the places had edges. She said nothing. \'93When did you notice the scar healing?\'94\
Maile shrugged and grimaced. \'93One morning I\'92m frying up breakfast and I think I\'92m imagining things. But it kept getting better.\'94\
Kathy examined it again, running her palm up and down the redness. She felt no ridges; Maile\'92s skin was as smooth as a polished opal, and almost as firm.\
